## The Proposal moving forward

Cypress internally knows and understands which command is current and how it is chained.

It could detect that if you "broke" the chain and tried to chain off of a previously finished chain of commands it would error.

```js
var address = '123 Main Street'

// chainer1 (subject is window)
cy.visit('http://localhost:8080/creditApp/index') 

// chainer2 (subject is <#address1>)
var addressInput = cy.get('#address1') 

// still chainer2 - this would not break since the `type`
// is the next command and its attached to the previous
// subject is still <#address1>
addressInput.type(address) 

// chainer3 (subject is now <#zip>)
var zip = cy.get('#zip')

// still chainer3 - same principle as above
zip.click()

addressInput.should('have.value', address) // BOOM

// we can now give you a great error message. Why? 
// because you've taken chainer2 and added a 
// new command to it - cy.should()
// we can detect this because the last chainer
// to run was chainer3
//
// the internals of cypress only carry 1 subject forward
// at a time (because everything in the DOM is mutable)
// and what actually happens is that the .should('have.value')
// would receive the subject of <#zip> and not <#address>
//
// chaining is all about relationships, but subjects simply 
// flow from one command to the next because we don't
// enable you to 'break up' chains of commands

```

